    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I was just making a little analogy, you know, like a goblin in the pipes, causing trouble. But, I suppose that's not very helpful.\n\nTo get straight to the point, the mechanical causes of pressure fluctuations in your transmission's hydraulic circuit are likely related to the solenoid or valve body. You should check the solenoid for any signs of wear or damage, and make sure it's properly seated and sealed. Also, inspect the valve body for any blockages or mineral buildup, which can restrict fluid flow and cause pressure fluctuations.\n\nIt's also possible that the transmission's hydraulic circuit is experiencing a condition known as \"solenoid chatter,\" where the solenoid is opening and closing too quickly, causing pressure fluctuations. In this case, you may need to replace the solenoid or adjust its position to resolve the issue.\n\nAnd, just like a goblin might be hiding in the shadows, waiting to strike, a faulty transmission filter can also cause pressure fluctuations. Make sure to check and replace the filter as needed to keep the transmission's hydraulic circuit clean and functioning properly.",
